--TEST--
A channel's capacity is a bound on the sender, not a hint: the producer never gets more than capacity-plus-one ahead of the consumer, and it does get ahead, so neither a growing buffer nor a rendezvous passes — `rule:concurrency/nothing-is-still-running-when-a-call-returns` over `nvs_stdlib::channel`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/core-task-channel-t-a-bounded-queue-between-tasks
// The neighbouring case pins that a capacity-one channel loses and reorders
// nothing. What it cannot see is *why* the sender waited, because at capacity
// one every send after the first waits and a rendezvous would look identical.
// This case is the bound asserted on both sides
// (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): a channel that grew instead of suspending
// lets the producer finish first and the lead reaches 20; a channel that
// handed off only to a waiting receiver never lets the lead exceed one.
class Flow {
    public static int $sent = 0;
    public static int $received = 0;
    public static int $maxLead = 0;
}

// Capacity two, so the two failures above sit on opposite sides of the
// answer rather than both above or both below it.
var $chan = new Core\Task\Channel<int>(2);

Core\Task::all({
    produce: fn(): int => {
        foreach (Core\Arr::range(1, 20) as int $n) {
            $chan->send($n);
            Flow::$sent += 1;
            var $lead = Flow::$sent - Flow::$received;
            if ($lead > Flow::$maxLead) {
                Flow::$maxLead = $lead;
            }
        }
        // Without this the consumer's `foreach` never ends and the group
        // cannot return, which is `close` being the only end-of-stream signal.
        $chan->close();
        return Flow::$sent;
    },
    consume: fn(): int => {
        foreach ($chan as int $n) {
            Flow::$received += 1;
        }
        return Flow::$received;
    },
});

echo Flow::$sent, " sent, ", Flow::$received, " received", "\n";

// Upper side: the sender was suspended by the capacity rather than allowed to
// run the whole range into a buffer that grew.
if (Flow::$maxLead <= 3) {
    echo "bounded by the capacity", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the buffer grew to ", Flow::$maxLead, "\n";
}

// Lower side: it is a buffer and not a rendezvous — the producer really did
// get ahead of a consumer that had not asked for the value yet.
if (Flow::$maxLead > 1) {
    echo "buffered, not a rendezvous", "\n";
} else {
    echo "handed off one at a time", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
20 sent, 20 received
bounded by the capacity
buffered, not a rendezvous
